      "llm_findings": [
        {
          "id": "llm-1",
          "file_path": "src/shop/checkout.py",
          "start_line": 8,
          "end_line": 8,
          "category": "data handling",
          "severity": "high",
          "summary": "finalise() now passes a minor-unit (cents) value into apply_credit, which is documented to take whole currency units, inflating the recorded credit by 100x.",
          "explanation": "The changed line calls apply_credit(order[\"invoice\"], order[\"cents\"]). The callee's own contract, stated in its docstring, is explicit: \"Apply a credit given in whole currency units (not cents).\" The field name being passed is the cents/minor-unit field, so the invoice's credit is set to a value 100 times the intended amount (e.g. an order of 10.00 stores credit 1000 rather than 10). The previous argument, order[\"amount\"], matched the callee's units contract. Secondary concern from the same line: the repository's order-construction code (src/shop/orders.py:8, create_order) builds an order dict containing only the key \"total\" and no \"cents\" key, so order[\"cents\"] is not an established order field in this codebase and this indexing can raise KeyError instead of the intended credit application; the units contract violation alone is enough to reject the line.",
          "evidence": "src/shop/checkout.py:8 -> `apply_credit(order[\"invoice\"], order[\"cents\"])`; src/shop/invoices.py:23-25 -> `def apply_credit(invoice, amount, note=\"\"):` / `\"\"\"Apply a credit given in whole currency units (not cents).\"\"\"` / `invoice[\"credit\"] = amount`; src/shop/orders.py:6-8 -> `def create_order(net, region):` / `\"\"\"Create an order.\"\"\"` / `return {\"total\": order_total(net, region)}`.",
          "suggested_fix": "Pass a whole-currency-unit amount as apply_credit expects, e.g. convert first: `from .money import from_cents` then `apply_credit(order[\"invoice\"], from_cents(order[\"cents\"]))`, or keep/resolve an order field that is already in whole currency units (such as the order total) and pass that.",
          "source": "llm",
